From: HardDinD developers
Subject: um: publish guest TSC timekeeping to a read-only vvar

Choose design (a). A fitted TSC-to-host-MONOTONIC conversion cannot stay
exact across host NTP updates. Instead, harddind_vdso_clock=1 registers an
invariant Intel TSC clocksource for BOTH the guest syscall and vDSO paths.
The default remains the host-MONOTONIC timer clocksource and syscall vDSO.
Require host clocksource=tsc, invariant TSC, and CPUID 15/16 frequency data;
otherwise fail closed to the original timer. Frequency discovery follows
the pinned kernel's native_calibrate_tsc, without timed calibration.
Seed wall time and the monotonic epoch from the host. A kernel
thread disciplines the guest through ADJ_FREQUENCY: RAW-to-host-MONOTONIC
rate estimates track host NTP and TSC calibration error; bounded frequency
correction slews phase without stepping MONOTONIC. Propagate only changes
to host REALTIME-minus-MONOTONIC through ADJ_SETOFFSET. Both syscall and vDSO
observe the generic timekeeper's atomic updates. harddind_clock_sync=0
allows A/B and independent guest NTP; synchronization defaults on with TSC.
Poll every 100 ms with a one-second phase time constant. A 32-second poll
measured 575 us of error across a real 23 ppm host rate change; direct rate
tracking avoids the extra lag from smoothing. Each sample uses host vDSO
reads, and the guest vDSO remains entirely local to the generic timekeeper.
Discard cross timestamps spanning over 5 us. /sys/kernel/harddind_clock is a
read-only live cross timestamp, including when discipline is disabled, for
independent drift checks without host privileges or a network time server.

Design (b) was rejected: exec gives each broker a host vDSO/vvar, but
init_new_context and cached-broker scrubbing unmap everything below
STUB_START (including the randomized host mapping). Retaining it requires
relocation above TASK_SIZE, ELF discovery, and preserving the vvar relative
layout. A constant offset also misses guest NTP and guest coarse clocks;
a trapped host-vDSO fallback would already return guest time, making an
unconditional post-call offset wrong. No runner/stub/filter changes here.

Use the generic timekeeper update and namespace datastore, not tick-time
sampling. Its seqcounts publish base/mult/shift/cycle_last atomically
before the syscall timekeeper becomes visible. settimeofday/adjtimex,
clocksource changes and coarse time updates use the same publication.
Use the generic timens layout: the first page holds immutable namespace
offsets and the next page holds real timekeeper data. Apply the kernel's
offset table after interpolation (MONOTONIC, RAW, COARSE and BOOTTIME;
REALTIME remains unshifted). Generic vdso_join_timens zaps the mapping on
fork/setns so the next access refaults the correct namespace and real pages.
Check the real page's clock mode to preserve the boot gate in every
namespace. Unknown clocks, unavailable mode and oversized/backwards deltas
still trap. Vvar has no VM_MAYWRITE and is sealed; CLONE_VM shares read-only
pages, and broker reuse refaults the newly installed guest mappings.

